  "worked_solution": "Notice that the logarithms share the same base.\n\nFirst, we apply the power rule to the *second* term to make the coefficient in front of each logarithm the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(72x)+2{log}_{3}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(72x)+{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we combine the logarithms using the product rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(72x\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(72{x}^{3}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in its equivalent exponential form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(72{x}^{3})} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 72{x}^{3} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(72\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡36+{log}_{3}⁡{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡36+{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(9) & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=\\frac{1}{2}$ only.\"",
